theorem average-below-an-end
  metamath    avglt2
  note        The average of A and B lies below B by half of B − A, so it is
              below B exactly when A is.
  let A ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let B ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  then A < B ↔ (A + B)/2 < B

1.  A < B ↔ (A + B)/2 < B
    both directions

    direction
    assume A < B                                                      (D1)

    1.1.  (A + B)/2 < B
          inequalities
            A < B              D1
            A + B < B + B      adding B
            (A + B)/2 < B      multiplying by 1/2

    direction
    assume (A + B)/2 < B                                              (D2)

    1.2.  A < B
          inequalities
            (A + B)/2 < B      D2
            A + B < 2B         multiplying by 2
            A < B              adding −B
